@ExtendWith(MockitoExtension.class)
class CustomerServiceTest {
    @Mock CustomerRepository customerRepository;
    @InjectMocks CustomerService customerService; // real CustomerService instance

    @Test
    void findCustomer_whenExists_shouldReturnCustomer() {
        Customer c = new Customer(1L, "Alice");
        when(customerRepository.findById(1L)).thenReturn(Optional.of(c));

        Customer result = customerService.findCustomer(1L);

        assertEquals("Alice", result.getName());
        verify(customerRepository).findById(1L);
    }
}
```

```java
@WebMvcTest(CustomerController.class)
class CustomerControllerTest {
    @Autowired MockMvc mockMvc;
    @MockBean CustomerService customerService;

    @Test
    void getCustomer_whenExists_shouldReturn200() throws Exception {
        when(customerService.find(1L)).thenReturn(new CustomerDTO(1L, "Alice"));

        mockMvc.perform(get("/api/customers/1"))
            .andExpect(status().isOk())
            .andExpect(jsonPath("$.name").value("Alice"));
    }
}
```

```java
@DataJpaTest
class CustomerRepositoryTest {
    @Autowired CustomerRepository repository;
    @Autowired TestEntityManager em;

    @Test
    void findByEmail_whenExists_shouldReturnCustomer() {
        em.persist(new Customer("alice@example.com", "Alice"));
        em.flush();

        Optional<Customer> result = repository.findByEmail("alice@example.com");

        assertTrue(result.isPresent());
        assertEquals("Alice", result.get().getName());
    }
}
```

@Mock is a pure Mockito annotation that creates a mock object without involving Spring at all. It works with @ExtendWith(MockitoExtension.class) and is fast because no application context is created. This is the right choice for unit tests where you instantiate the class under test directly with @InjectMocks.

@MockBean is a Spring Boot Test annotation that does two things: it creates a Mockito mock and it registers that mock as a bean in the Spring application context, replacing any existing real bean of that type. This means you must be loading a Spring context for @MockBean to make sense — typically when your test class is annotated with @SpringBootTest or a slice annotation like @WebMvcTest.

The key practical difference is performance and scope. @Mock tests are fast because there is no Spring context. @MockBean tests are slower because loading the application context takes time, and each unique combination of @MockBean declarations may trigger a separate context load.

A concrete example: when testing a Controller through @WebMvcTest, you want to mock the Service layer — you use @MockBean because Spring MVC needs a real context to process HTTP requests. When unit testing that same Service in isolation, you use @Mock because you just need the service instantiated with its dependencies mocked out.
